<TopicPage slug="subjects/anthropology/foundational/culture-kin-long-view">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Anthropology studies humans twice over: as <strong>biological animals</strong> (evolution,
    bones, genes) and as <strong>culture-bearing beings</strong> (kinship, ritual, meaning). Its
    founding insight is that the second is as natural as the first — every human alive is born
    into webs of kinship and symbols that shape what they eat, marry and believe. The timeline
    below is the whole story in twelve frames.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="300,000 years on one scrubber — from savannah to Moon" widget="timeline" config={deepTimeline} />

  <h2>What culture does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It solves problems without genes.</strong> Techniques, tools and norms accumulate across generations — culture is humanity's second inheritance system, and it moves far faster than DNA.</li>
    <li><strong>It defines kin.</strong> Every society classifies relatives, but the systems differ radically — who counts as "brother", who one may marry, who inherits. Kinship is society's first grammar.</li>
    <li><strong>It universalises by differing.</strong> Every culture has music, marriage rules, mourning rituals and stories — yet the forms vary endlessly. The constants are the field's map; the variations are its data.</li>
    <li><strong>It deserves study on its own terms.</strong> The discipline's hard-won method — ethnography — asks what a practice means <em>from inside</em> before judging it from outside.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Notice the timeline's shape: 285,000 years of foraging, then everything we call "civilisation"
    in the last 5%. We are a hunting species with a slightly longer memory — and an adolescent
    technology.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hublin et al., Nature (2017) on Jebel Irhoud; Harari-style syntheses checked against E. Scott, "A Deeper Science"; standard anthropological literature.</p>
